          %h1#ech_homotopy Čech homotopy
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea_and_discussion"} Idea and discussion
              %li
                %a{:href => "#handling_the_problem_of_coherence"} Handling the problem of coherence
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#giving_homotopy_coherence_directly"} Giving homotopy coherence directly:
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#using_the_vietoris_complex"} Using the Vietoris complex:
              %li
                %a{:href => "#homotopy_groups_or_homotopy_progroups"} Homotopy groups or homotopy progroups?
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
          %h2#idea_and_discussion Idea and discussion
          %p
            In Čech cohomology, in its
            %em traditional
            form, you resolve the space by an inverse system or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check{C}(X,-)
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^n(-,A)
            at this pro-object. As cohomology switches ‘variance’ we get a
            %em direct system
            or
            %em ind-object
            of Abelian groups, and usually we then take its (direct) limit (i.e. its colimit) to get
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check{H}^n(X,A)
          %p
            Now dualise … . Replace cohomology by homotopy
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A,-]
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A,\check{C}(X,-)]
            is a pro-object and so we might want to take limits not colimits to get the thing we want, but
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim
            is not an exact functor on the usual categories available here, such as groups, sets, modules, etc.
          %p
            There is another problem, coherence!
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check{C}(X,-)
            is a pro-object in the homotopy category of simplicial sets, not in the category of simplicial sets itself. It is a homotopy coherent system, but the choices of covering refinement maps cause the problem.
          %p
            We are very near the topics discussed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+methods"} Čech methods
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+shape+theory"} strong shape theory
            (when that has been written!). One interpretation of Čech homotopy is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
          %h2#handling_the_problem_of_coherence Handling the problem of coherence
          %h3#giving_homotopy_coherence_directly Giving homotopy coherence directly:
          %p
            As explained in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+methods"} Čech methods
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            of a space
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a refinement
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            of
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            for a given set in
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            there may be many different sets in
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            containing it, so the seemingly natural way to get a simplicial map
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check{C}(X,\alpha)\to \check{C}(X,\beta)
          %p
            involves a choice. We choose a ‘refinement mapping’
            %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo :
                  %mi α
                  %mo →
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi : \alpha \to \beta
            so that for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\in \alpha
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_f0fb655cf193ca709f3ce8e7497e67ab44ac03f4_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
